WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.220
Ben Wattenberg: Hello.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.370
I’m Ben Wattenberg.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:12.580
Russia is in turmoil: war in Chechnya, a stagnant
economy, a faltering democracy.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:17.120
But does trouble for Russia mean trouble for
America?

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.900
Helping us to answer that question are Leon
Aron, resident scholar at the American Enterprise

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:29.230
Institute and author of a forthcoming biography
of Boris Yeltsin; Peter Rodman, former national

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:34.500
security aide under Presidents Reagan and
Bush and the author of a book about the Cold

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:40.360
War, “More Precious Than Peace,” he is
currently at the Nixon Center for Peace and

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:47.260
Freedom; Peter Rutland, a professor of government
at Wesleyan University and author of “The

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:53.650
Politics of Economic Stagnation in the Soviet
Union”; and Paula Dobriansky, former director

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:58.010
of European and Soviet affairs for the National
Security Council under President Reagan and

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.900
now an adjunct fellow at the Hudson Institute.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:06.610
The topic before this house: Is Russia a threat
again?

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:23.250
This week on “Think Tank.”

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:28.979
For 40 years, from an American perspective,
the Soviet Union was the most important foreign

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:29.979
country.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:31.600
Then the Soviet Union split apart.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.290
The years following the collapse of communism
have not been easy for Russia.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:45.410
When the Berlin Wall came down, people on
both sides of the former Iron Curtain rejoiced.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.790
But today’s mood is much more sober.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:54.030
Russia’s gross domestic product has been
shrinking, by 19 percent in 1992 and by 12

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.409
percent in 1993.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:00.740
Inflation currently runs at 18 percent per
month.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:01.740
And crime is increasing.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:08.780
The so-called Russian mafia controls an estimated
40 percent of all transactions in goods and

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.780
services.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.959
Last December, Russian troops invaded the
breakaway republic of Chechnya, bombing its

00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:17.110
capital of Grozny to rubble.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:23.110
The death toll has reached a reported 24,000
civilians.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:28.620
Former republics like Ukraine and Kazakhstan
fear that Moscow might turn expansionist and

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:34.220
try to rebuild the Soviet empire through coercion
or even outright invasion.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:39.880
It is said that President Boris Yeltsin, once
called the George Washington of Russian democracy,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:45.870
is now overwhelmed by the job and suffers
from ill health, perhaps alcoholism.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:52.160
Many Russian democrats, like former Prime
Minister Yegor Gaidar, have turned against

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.160
Yeltsin.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:59.070
Hard-line nationalist parties are believed
to be rising in popularity and influence.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:04.680
And while Soviet communism has been delegated
to the dustbin of history, Russia still has

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:11.069
thousands of nuclear weapons capable of hitting
Western Europe and the United States.

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:18.810
It used to be, gentlemen and lady, that the
views on the Soviet Union were divided into

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:25.140
hawks and doves, and now it seems to be that
they are divided between optimists and pessimists.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.370
And I just wonder, just to sort of establish
where you all are, maybe we’ll just go quickly

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:30.410
around the horn here.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.920
Starting with Leon Aron, if you could just
try to tell me where you are.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.650
Leon Aron: Ben, I think I am a very cautious
optimist.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:42.160
I think it’s a very cold spring.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:48.690
We just had a big chill with Chechnya and
perhaps frost, but it’s still a sowing season.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.070
Ben Wattenberg: Okay.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.070
Peter Rutland.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.260
Peter Rutland: I’m also an optimist in the
long term, although I wouldn’t underestimate

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.850
the problems in the short term.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:00.310
In terms of a threat, Russia may be a threat
to its own people more than to people beyond

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.310
its own borders.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:02.450
Ben Wattenberg: Paula Dobriansky.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.780
Paula Dobriansky: In the short term, I’m
a pessimist, specifically because of the events

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.019
in Chechnya.

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:13.470
The fact that Moscow has waged such barbaric
actions in dealing with the circumstances

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.670
do not bode well for the future.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.640
Lawlessness, corruption is still prevalent.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.350
The legacy of communism still has not been
broken.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:26.600
In the long term, maybe a cautious optimist;
in the short term, a pessimist.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.150
Ben Wattenberg: Peter Rodman.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.810
Peter Rodman: I’m a pessimist.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.300
Russia has always been a problem.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:38.880
I mean, the history of Europe teaches that
Russia has always been a big, clumsy, difficult,

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.169
powerful factor.

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:41.940
And I think we’re seeing that again.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.370
I wouldn’t call Russia a threat.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.770
I think it’s going to be a major problem.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:49.800
I see some disturbing trends in its foreign
policy, and I think we need to be more realistic

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.060
about it than we have been.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.420
Ben Wattenberg: Why do people say — and
it is said — that Russia has always been

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.420
a problem?

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.420
I mean, is it their geography?

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.000
Is it their psychology?

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Is it their history?

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.840
I mean, Leon, you grew up in Russia.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.220
Leon Aron: Yes.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.310
Well, it certainly was a problem for me as
I was growing up.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.310
[Laughter.]

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:13.640
But — well, I think there are a number of
things, Ben: its positioning sort of between

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:21.229
Europe and Asia; its tradition of authoritarianism;
its sort of inability to decide whether they

00:05:21.229 --> 00:05:26.940
belong to Europe, with Enlightenment and free
enterprise and democracy, or whether they

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:35.389
belong to Asia; and its also perennial — almost
perennial insecurity, which largely was brought

00:05:35.389 --> 00:05:40.889
about by its own expansion, that led Russia
to this paradoxical situation that the more

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:42.780
expanded, the more insecure it became.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.639
Peter Rutland: I’m not sure we should say
it’s a problem.

00:05:45.639 --> 00:05:49.110
I’m not sure that any country be described
as a problem.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.199
Sure, Russia has had a difficult history.

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:57.930
But at various points in history, it’s pulled
Europe’s bacon out of the fire; it’s saved

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:03.490
Europe from sundry dictators, from Napoleon
to Hitler.

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:06.440
And so it’s been a cycle of successes and
defeats.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:09.710
And Russia right now is at the depths of a
trough.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:13.460
But it will come back, and I don’t think
we should say that it’s a demonic country

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:14.460
that’s doomed to cause problems.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:15.520
Ben Wattenberg: Well, will it come back as
a democracy?

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.120
Paula Dobriansky: Well, I think one of the
greatest concerns today, especially, is the

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:25.680
fact that democracy has not taken hold in
the sense of the term in Russia and especially

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.400
that those countries in Central and Eastern
Europe are very concerned about Russia’s

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.400
intentions.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.400
They’re unclear.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.289
Take Ukraine, for example.

00:06:34.289 --> 00:06:38.630
Ukraine is very concerned about the events
in Chechnya, especially because Russia has

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:45.190
not really, in all intents and purposes, fully
acknowledged the sovereignty and the existence

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:46.810
of Ukraine.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:51.810
So the dust has not settled, and in that sense,
it does pose a problem, not only for us, but

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:52.810
for the Europeans.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.940
Peter Rutland: But, Paula, you say Russia
isn’t a democracy.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:59.090
But five years ago, would you have expected
Russia to be this democratic?

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:00.220
Paula Dobriansky: No.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.290
I think that it has made certain strides.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:03.520
That is true.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.850
But still, it is not a democracy in the truest
sense of the term.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:11.680
Clearly, the actions undertaken by Moscow
in dealing with the situation in Chechnya

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.009
defy any semblance of democratic, peaceful
actions.

00:07:16.009 --> 00:07:19.030
Leon Aron: With one exception, Paula.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:23.139
It seems to me that indeed Chechnya was a
test for Russian democracy.

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:24.330
The executive failed completely.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.330
Paula Dobriansky: They failed it.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:26.330
Leon Aron: The executive failed.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:27.810
However, there is one silver lining.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:28.810
Ben Wattenberg: Wait, wait.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.720
The United States — I don’t want to sound
as if I’m pro the Russian government, but

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.580
the United States was a democracy when we
engaged in a civil war dealing sort of with

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.160
a, quotes, “breakaway republic.”

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:43.590
I mean, it’s not, just on its surface, an
outrageous behavior.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.620
Leon Aron: No, I don’t think so.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:50.199
And, you know, the Chechnya regime itself,
the Dudayev regime, was far from democratic

00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:51.720
and blameless for all this.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:53.310
But it failed in its brutality.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.610
It failed in its ineptness.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:57.639
It’s failed in the amount of casualties
that it inflicted.

00:07:57.639 --> 00:08:01.940
More importantly, I think it failed in the
way that war was presented to the people and

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:03.840
the way that the executive worked with the
parliament.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:04.910
Ben Wattenberg: Peter Rodman.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:08.460
Peter Rodman: The question you asked was “Why
is Russia a problem?”

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.690
And I think Leon described the historical
reasons very well.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.479
But I would emphasize one factor, which is
its power.

00:08:14.479 --> 00:08:20.940
It is a huge concentration of power in the
middle of the Eurasian landmass, and it has

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:26.320
always been a problem for anybody who had
the misfortune to live in its immediate neighborhood.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:27.750
And as Leon said, it’s been clumsy.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:32.649
It’s never been quite sure where it fit
into the sort of international system.

00:08:32.649 --> 00:08:33.649
And I think —

00:08:33.649 --> 00:08:34.649
Peter Rutland: Sort of like Germany.

00:08:34.649 --> 00:08:35.649
Peter Rodman: Exactly, like Germany.

00:08:35.649 --> 00:08:39.899
And it’s not an accident that the history
of the last — this century has been, you

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:45.291
know, Germany and Russia either competing
or taking turns disrupting the peace.

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:49.239
And we may — you know, I worry that we may
be heading in — you know, right now the

00:08:49.239 --> 00:08:51.949
balance of power in Europe is benign.

00:08:51.949 --> 00:08:55.709
But I think a lot of that is because the United
States is sitting there as the stabilizer.

00:08:55.709 --> 00:09:01.649
I mean, this gets into the foreign policy
concern for us, which is how do we, you know,

00:09:01.649 --> 00:09:04.319
stabilize the situation, make sure that it
doesn’t become a threat.

00:09:04.319 --> 00:09:07.319
Because as I said before, it’s not a threat;
it’s a problem.

00:09:07.319 --> 00:09:11.170
Ben Wattenberg: Why did de Tocqueville, when
he came here, I guess in the 1830s, say that

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:15.899
the two great nations of the future were going
to be the United States and Russia?

00:09:15.899 --> 00:09:16.899
What did he sense then?

00:09:16.899 --> 00:09:17.899
Was it physical?

00:09:17.899 --> 00:09:20.819
Peter Rodman: It was physical power that was
dormant and yet was inevitable.

00:09:20.819 --> 00:09:26.180
Ben Wattenberg: If you were writing about
the world today, say, for the next hundred

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.249
years, would you say those are the two big
powers still?

00:09:28.249 --> 00:09:31.569
Peter Rodman: No, it’s more — there are
more, other players — Germany, Japan —

00:09:31.569 --> 00:09:32.569
Peter Rutland: China.

00:09:32.569 --> 00:09:33.569
Paula Dobriansky: China.

00:09:33.569 --> 00:09:35.040
Peter Rodman: And China, absolutely.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:38.850
So it’s — you know, it’s been evolving
into a, you know, more multipolar system.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:43.689
But they’re still — I mean, Russia is
big, and particularly because it’s in turmoil,

00:09:43.689 --> 00:09:47.160
it is scary to anybody who lives in its immediate
neighborhood.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:53.350
Leon Aron: Well, just continuing with that
theme, it seems to me that when I talk about

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:58.830
the silver lining in this Chechnya affair,
one thing that it did show is that the Russian

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:05.699
society is no longer synonymous with the Kremlin,
that the people did not buy the party line

00:10:05.699 --> 00:10:12.480
and, most importantly, the media were completely
free to report something that in its explicitness

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:19.350
and candor, I would say, was probably unequaled
by any country at war, except for that of

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:20.540
the United States during Vietnam.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:27.540
I mean, the coverage that you saw on national
television during the Chechen war utterly

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:31.790
belied the notion that this is an authoritarian
regime in that particular regard, that it

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:35.100
could crack down on the media whenever it
wanted.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:40.420
So it seems to me that while the executive,
I repeat, failed miserably in this war, we

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:45.769
no longer could equate the Kremlin with Russia,
something that is utterly new and, in my view,

00:10:45.769 --> 00:10:46.769
quite optimistic.

00:10:46.769 --> 00:10:49.290
Paula Dobriansky: Leon, I would agree with
your point.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:50.910
Absolutely, that is the silver lining.

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:56.889
My reason before for mentioning why I don’t
see Russia at this time being a democracy

00:10:56.889 --> 00:11:00.829
in the fullest sense of the term is because,
yes, there have been free and fair elections.

00:11:00.829 --> 00:11:05.529
Yes, as you mentioned the silver lining, an
important factor in the crisis in dealing

00:11:05.529 --> 00:11:09.660
with Chechnya has been the fact that there
haven’t been these constraints on media,

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:14.050
that there has been a vibrant media, and in
fact, public opinion has gone very much against

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:15.709
the actions of Moscow.

00:11:15.709 --> 00:11:20.199
However, there still is not an independent
functioning judiciary.

00:11:20.199 --> 00:11:24.540
There is no rule of law, which is a bedrock
of any society.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:26.119
That has not taken hold.

00:11:26.119 --> 00:11:33.449
And secondly, although I think the population
has certainly become more engaged in democratic

00:11:33.449 --> 00:11:39.420
ideas and values, the understanding and the
grasp of it and its translation into concrete

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.139
action, it still needs to evolve.

00:11:42.139 --> 00:11:43.139
Much needs to be done.

00:11:43.139 --> 00:11:44.139
Ben Wattenberg: Let me ask you a question.

00:11:44.139 --> 00:11:45.779
What about the commercial realm?

00:11:45.779 --> 00:11:50.249
We said in the setup piece that 40 percent
of the Soviet economy is run by the mafia.

00:11:50.249 --> 00:11:56.519
But that mafia, or earlier the black market,
I mean, that can be viewed as sort of a rudimentary

00:11:56.519 --> 00:11:58.160
stage of real capitalism.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.720
You let all the illegal enterprises flourish,
and then you regulate them.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:02.749
I mean, is that plausible?

00:12:02.749 --> 00:12:06.209
Peter Rutland: I think if we look from the
robber barons in American history to the current

00:12:06.209 --> 00:12:10.679
situation in China, we can see that this sort
of informal economy, to use a more polite

00:12:10.679 --> 00:12:15.000
term than “mafias,” that the two shade
into one another.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.359
The informal economy is rife in every economy
in the world.

00:12:18.359 --> 00:12:23.269
And in some economies, it’s more prevalent
than others; in some economies, it’s legislators

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:24.980
that are getting shot down on the streets.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:27.009
Peter Rodman: We did have our robber baron
period.

00:12:27.009 --> 00:12:29.439
Ben Wattenberg: We also had people selling
protection in the United States.

00:12:29.439 --> 00:12:32.869
Peter Rodman: Well, but that’s a local aberration.

00:12:32.869 --> 00:12:35.100
We also had a legal system and a political
system.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:43.769
And as our society evolved, the legal system
was able to dominate these — for the most

00:12:43.769 --> 00:12:44.920
part, these criminal elements.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:48.449
I mean, Russia doesn’t have a legal system
or an established political system.

00:12:48.449 --> 00:12:52.769
Everything is in turmoil at the same time,
and there’s no guarantee it’s going to

00:12:52.769 --> 00:12:56.179
evolve smoothly into a nice, bourgeois, you
know, law-abiding society.

00:12:56.179 --> 00:12:58.319
Ben Wattenberg: But it has a president.

00:12:58.319 --> 00:13:02.170
And Leon, you are writing a biography about
Mr. Yeltsin.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:06.399
And I guess the popular question is: Is he
a Democrat or a drunk?

00:13:06.399 --> 00:13:07.399
And I wonder —

00:13:07.399 --> 00:13:08.399
Leon Aron: Or both.

00:13:08.399 --> 00:13:09.399
Ben Wattenberg: Or both, right.

00:13:09.399 --> 00:13:10.399
Leon Aron: He could be both at the same time.

00:13:10.399 --> 00:13:12.950
History is replete with Democrats who have
dabbled into all kinds of diversions.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:16.199
Ben Wattenberg: Where do you come out on your
general view of Yeltsin?

00:13:16.199 --> 00:13:25.410
Leon Aron: Well, I think Yeltsin is a man
who is generally in favor of democratic development,

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:29.749
but who himself obviously does not have the
experience being the party boss all his life,

00:13:29.749 --> 00:13:36.309
doesn’t have the experience or very much
understanding of the free market or democracy.

00:13:36.309 --> 00:13:39.309
And I think he was an ideal person for the
barricades.

00:13:39.309 --> 00:13:43.949
Ben Wattenberg: But it must have been a great
temptation during Chechnya for such a man

00:13:43.949 --> 00:13:45.980
to close down the press, and yet he didn’t.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:47.130
Leon Aron: That’s precisely it.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:52.919
And not only that, but in his address to the
nation, he reaffirmed that with all his problems

00:13:52.919 --> 00:14:00.489
that he has with the media, he still will
not allow any sort of crackdown on the media.

00:14:00.489 --> 00:14:06.059
So I think he also — unfortunately — like
Gorbachev, is a man whose hour has passed.

00:14:06.059 --> 00:14:07.809
I mean, he won the revolution.

00:14:07.809 --> 00:14:09.980
There is no doubt about that.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:16.360
But Russia now moved beyond the barricades,
and in this space between the revolution and

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.160
democracy, Yeltsin is lost.

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:20.389
He could not find his proper niche.

00:14:20.389 --> 00:14:25.980
And I think part of it, part of the reason
he decided on this utterly disastrous — politically,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:30.449
morally, and militarily — action in Chechnya
is because he was searching for a mission

00:14:30.449 --> 00:14:32.319
and he was searching for a way to establish
himself.

00:14:32.319 --> 00:14:36.720
Peter Rutland: The question I would like to
come back to is whether Yeltsin, if he leaves

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:38.980
the scene, can be replaced.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:42.319
Is he actually pivotal to the way that the
political elite works in Russia?

00:14:42.319 --> 00:14:46.679
And that’s the thing that worries me, that
there may not be a clear transition to another

00:14:46.679 --> 00:14:47.679
—

00:14:47.679 --> 00:14:53.970
Ben Wattenberg: If they go to chaos and anarchy
in Russia, is this cause for America to worry?

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:59.189
I mean, is that — is this a huge concern
to us, with the nukes rattling around and

00:14:59.189 --> 00:15:00.189
—

00:15:00.189 --> 00:15:01.189
Peter Rodman: Well, you put your finger on
it.

00:15:01.189 --> 00:15:02.589
The nukes are the main concern.

00:15:02.589 --> 00:15:03.589
And anarchy —

00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:04.589
Ben Wattenberg: How many are there?

00:15:04.589 --> 00:15:07.660
Peter Rodman: Well, there were 20,000, I think,
before the — and there are arms control

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:10.199
— arms reduction agreements that are bringing
it down under 10,000.

00:15:10.199 --> 00:15:11.230
Ben Wattenberg: Warheads.

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:12.269
Peter Rodman: Warheads.

00:15:12.269 --> 00:15:16.589
And that process is going on, and we have
a stake in it.

00:15:16.589 --> 00:15:20.440
Now, so far, the Russians have had very good
command and control over their nuclear weapons.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:26.029
But the continuing deterioration of their
political authority, you know, could change

00:15:26.029 --> 00:15:27.029
that.

00:15:27.029 --> 00:15:30.649
I mean, I think if Russia is weak, it obviously
addresses the concern I raised at the beginning.

00:15:30.649 --> 00:15:33.639
Then it’s less of a threat to its neighbors.

00:15:33.639 --> 00:15:38.089
On the other hand, there is the nuclear anarchy,
which is enormously dangerous.

00:15:38.089 --> 00:15:40.009
And secondly, I think there is an Aristotelian
cycle.

00:15:40.009 --> 00:15:43.959
I don’t think a great power endlessly tolerates
humiliation.

00:15:43.959 --> 00:15:46.949
I think there will be a return to some central
authority.

00:15:46.949 --> 00:15:53.079
I mean, you know, a strong man — I think
you’ll have — I think anarchy will inevitably

00:15:53.079 --> 00:15:59.170
produce some kind of authoritarian regime,
so we’ll be back maybe where we started.

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:03.959
Ben Wattenberg: Peter, when you say we might
go back to where we started, we’re not going

00:16:03.959 --> 00:16:05.389
to go back to the Soviet Union with —

00:16:05.389 --> 00:16:06.389
Peter Rodman: No, no, no.

00:16:06.389 --> 00:16:09.209
But in terms of its — we’re talking about
its internal evolution and, you know, whether

00:16:09.209 --> 00:16:10.339
we have the hopes for democracy.

00:16:10.339 --> 00:16:14.610
And I think if democracy turns into anarchy,
it usually ends up in some authoritarian —

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:19.179
Ben Wattenberg: But if it then goes retrograde,
we are not as bad off as we were during the

00:16:19.179 --> 00:16:20.359
Cold War in terms of the whole USSR and —

00:16:20.359 --> 00:16:23.769
Peter Rodman: Well, I think there is a big
difference between Russian foreign policy

00:16:23.769 --> 00:16:24.769
and Soviet foreign policy.

00:16:24.769 --> 00:16:25.790
I think there is a big improvement.

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:27.610
I mean, Russia is a state.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:31.419
I think it has a sense of its national interest,
or it’s groping for a sense of its national

00:16:31.419 --> 00:16:32.669
interest.

00:16:32.669 --> 00:16:37.399
It doesn’t have this Leninist ideological
thrust to disrupt the Western position all

00:16:37.399 --> 00:16:38.399
around the world.

00:16:38.399 --> 00:16:41.129
So even I, who worry about it, I can see a
big difference.

00:16:41.129 --> 00:16:45.109
Paula Dobriansky: Peter, wouldn’t you agree,
though, that although you said that Russian

00:16:45.109 --> 00:16:50.339
foreign policy is not synonymous with Soviet
foreign policy, that nevertheless, one of

00:16:50.339 --> 00:16:57.919
the problems or one of the challenges which
we face is that Russia’s interests are not

00:16:57.919 --> 00:17:01.189
— or have not been in common with ours.

00:17:01.189 --> 00:17:07.620
When you look at the situation in Bosnia,
look at the situation in the Middle East of

00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:13.370
late, as well as in Europe the handling of
NATO, our interests or our directions have

00:17:13.370 --> 00:17:14.370
been rather divergent.

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:15.370
Ben Wattenberg: What have they done in the
Middle East?

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:18.520
Peter Rodman: They’re selling nuclear reactors
and submarines to Iran, which I think actually

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:23.000
bothers me more than Chechnya does, because
this impinges directly on an area of vital

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:24.000
interest to us.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:25.059
Paula Dobriansky: And this is one of the —

00:17:25.059 --> 00:17:26.059
Peter Rodman: I think —

00:17:26.059 --> 00:17:27.559
Paula Dobriansky: Excuse me, I was just going
to say, this is one of, I think, the greatest

00:17:27.559 --> 00:17:33.190
challenges that we face that’s separate
from what is evolving on the domestic scene,

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:39.549
that internationally, instead of our interests
being more parallel, that they have become

00:17:39.549 --> 00:17:41.059
divergent, and there is a polarization.

00:17:41.059 --> 00:17:46.450
Leon Aron: I think Paula and Peter have put
their fingers on it.

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:51.710
I think there are several aspects of the new
Russian policy that need to be taken into

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:52.710
consideration.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:57.210
I think to me, the Russian foreign policy,
globally — I’m not talking about the backyard;

00:17:57.210 --> 00:18:03.240
that’s a separate issue — globally, is
sort of like de Gaulle’s France.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:08.380
That is, you lose in substance what you try
to acquire in rhetoric, and you try to show,

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:13.000
at least on the rhetorical, symbolic level,
that you’re not in the US pocket.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.000
Ben Wattenberg: For example?

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.590
Leon Aron: For example, Bosnia is one example.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.220
Iran is another example.

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:23.380
Peter Rutland: The debate is pretty much within
the Cold War scheme of things, where security

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:25.679
is the dominant factor in foreign policy.

00:18:25.679 --> 00:18:29.170
I would suggest that economics is much more
important in the present world.

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:33.480
In economic terms, there are lots of mutual
interests between the West and Russia.

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:37.549
And the things about selling nuclear reactors
to Iran is purely economy.

00:18:37.549 --> 00:18:40.480
I mean, if the Russians don’t sell them
in five years’ time, the French or the Germans

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:41.480
will be selling them.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:44.330
Peter Rodman: No, but from our point of view,
it’s security, because of the Middle East

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:45.330
and the problem —

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:47.340
Peter Rutland: But from Russians’ point
of view, it’s economics.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.480
Ben Wattenberg: Let me ask a question.

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.840
How is the Clinton administration doing?

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:54.460
Have they handled this situation well?

00:18:54.460 --> 00:19:00.160
Paula Dobriansky: I don’t think that they
have handled it forcefully and directly.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:06.620
First of all, on the issue of Chechnya, the
comparison that this is similar to America’s

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:11.509
Civil War I don’t think was an appropriate
parallel.

00:19:11.509 --> 00:19:12.509
Secondly —

00:19:12.509 --> 00:19:13.539
Ben Wattenberg: Did the Clinton administration
make that parallel?

00:19:13.539 --> 00:19:14.539
Paula Dobriansky: Yes.

00:19:14.539 --> 00:19:17.480
The initial statement was that, well, this
is an internal matter and we should wipe our

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:18.639
hands of it.

00:19:18.639 --> 00:19:20.850
I don’t think it can be viewed as an internal
matter.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:23.809
It has ramifications for Russia’s development.

00:19:23.809 --> 00:19:30.460
It has ramifications for us from a security
standpoint, and it certainly has ramifications

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:31.679
for Eastern Europe.

00:19:31.679 --> 00:19:37.139
Secondly, and very significantly, I think
that the administration has put too many eggs

00:19:37.139 --> 00:19:42.180
in Yeltsin’s basket and also has placed
too much of an emphasis on a Russia-oriented

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:43.180
policy.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:48.779
Leon Aron: Well, I think Paula is right in
the sense that it should not be Russia only.

00:19:48.779 --> 00:19:51.440
But I think we would all agree, it should
be Russia first.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.500
And the reason for that is not any special
predilection for Russia, but the fact that

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:58.230
if Russia goes down, so will other countries.

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:01.610
Ben Wattenberg: And you have those nuclear
weapons around that nobody —

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:02.610
Leon Aron: That’s right.

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:04.850
Paula Dobriansky: If I may just jump in on
that, I don’t think I would say it should

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:09.390
be Russia first, because psychologically I
think that sends a very wrong signal.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:14.590
Secondly, many of these countries, just as
much as they are dependent upon the fate of

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:20.059
Russia, I think if you do not have democracy
in Ukraine, I think that is very much intertwined

00:20:20.059 --> 00:20:22.350
with the future of Russia if Ukraine does
not succeed.

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:25.759
Ben Wattenberg: Where do you come out on how
Clinton has done — or the Clinton administration?

00:20:25.759 --> 00:20:30.360
Peter Rutland: Well, I don’t think there
has been an effective policy, either a Russia-first

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:31.720
or a Ukraine-first policy.

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:35.600
I think there has been just a status in policy,
which in the long run will probably —

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:36.600
Ben Wattenberg: A what policy?

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:37.600
Peter Rutland: A status.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.049
There has been no real movement forward in
American policy.

00:20:40.049 --> 00:20:44.919
And in the long run, this is probably a good
thing, because if America had adopted a determined

00:20:44.919 --> 00:20:48.429
policy earlier on, it would probably have
been the wrong policy.

00:20:48.429 --> 00:20:51.080
So we’re better to just be an observer on
these processes.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:53.580
Ben Wattenberg: Peter Rodman, you have generally
been a critic of the Clinton foreign policy.

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:55.669
Peter Rodman: Yeah, I have been, and on this
one in particular.

00:20:55.669 --> 00:20:57.960
I see it in geopolitical terms.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:02.490
I think Russia — you need to hedge a possible
reemergence of Russia.

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:08.740
And yet I see — which means that if your
first line of defense is Russian policy, your

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:12.639
second line of defense is the independence
and security of all the new states that have

00:21:12.639 --> 00:21:15.880
emerged from the wreckage of the Soviet empire.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:22.169
And so I think we have a geopolitical stake
in Ukraine, in protecting the Eastern Europeans.

00:21:22.169 --> 00:21:28.559
And that’s why I think the disproportionate
foreign assistance that has gone to Russia

00:21:28.559 --> 00:21:29.559
is a geopolitical mistake.

00:21:29.559 --> 00:21:34.909
I mean, we have a stake in the independence
of all the others just in order to stabilize

00:21:34.909 --> 00:21:39.890
the present situation, and as a hedge against
the possible, you know, as I said, emergence

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:41.049
of Russia as a problem.

00:21:41.049 --> 00:21:43.250
Ben Wattenberg: We are running out of time.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:47.659
Let me just ask you one personal question.

00:21:47.659 --> 00:21:53.490
Five years ago, 10 years ago, the people who
dealt with the Cold War and the Soviet Union

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:55.820
were sort of at the top of the food chain
in Washington.

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:58.070
You know, that was what was really important.

00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:00.399
Now the Cold War is over.

00:22:00.399 --> 00:22:01.629
Do you find any difference?

00:22:01.629 --> 00:22:04.270
I mean, do people not return your phone calls?

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:08.179
Are you no longer invited to as many parties
or — [Laughter]

00:22:08.179 --> 00:22:12.960
Peter Rodman: No, no, but foreign policy as
a whole is less important.

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:15.510
And I worry about an isolationist mood in
the country.

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:22.379
Because I think if the United States removes
its stabilizing role or its stabilizing policy

00:22:22.379 --> 00:22:26.559
in the world, then I think all these problems
would end up bigger than they otherwise could

00:22:26.559 --> 00:22:27.559
be.

00:22:27.559 --> 00:22:33.380
Peter Rutland: Well, Ben, Henry Kissinger
said famously that power is the ultimate attraction,

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:34.380
right?

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:35.730
Ben Wattenberg: The “ultimate aphrodisiac”
is what he said.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:36.859
Peter Rutland: Aphrodisiac, right, exactly.

00:22:36.859 --> 00:22:38.899
I didn’t know if I was allowed to say something
like that.

00:22:38.899 --> 00:22:39.899
Ben Wattenberg: No, no, we’re allowed to.

00:22:39.899 --> 00:22:40.899
We have a very mature audience.

00:22:40.899 --> 00:22:41.899
[Laughter.]

00:22:41.899 --> 00:22:43.880
Peter Rutland: But I think Peter’s right,
though, you know, while the foreign policy

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:51.779
in general has changed, clearly I find in
retrospect that fear is also an ultimate attraction,

00:22:51.779 --> 00:22:57.640
and there is no doubt that the attention that’s
given to Russia and to those who are dealing

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:02.570
with Russia is not what is used to be and
— but you’re still inviting us, so we

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:03.570
must be doing something right.

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:07.120
Ben Wattenberg: Well, but we do more shows
about welfare and affirmative action and crime

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:11.149
than we probably would have done had this
program started five years ago or 10 years

00:23:11.149 --> 00:23:12.149
ago.

00:23:12.149 --> 00:23:13.149
Peter Rutland: Absolutely.

00:23:13.149 --> 00:23:15.909
Ben Wattenberg: All right, let me just as
a closing exercise here — again, we have

00:23:15.909 --> 00:23:23.320
to do it sort of briefly — picture the year
2002, which is when the — seven years from

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.860
now, when the budget is supposed to be balanced
here.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:27.750
What will the Soviet Union look like?

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:28.750
Leon Aron: Russia?

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:29.750
Ben Wattenberg: I’m sorry.

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:31.290
Leon Aron: I mean, that’s an easy question.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:32.290
[Laughter.]

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:33.290
Ben Wattenberg: Right.

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:34.290
What will Russia look like?

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:40.580
Leon Aron: I think it will still be a country
struggling economically and politically, but

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:45.690
less of a threat to its neighbors than at
any time in its 400-year history.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:49.299
Peter Rutland: I think Russia will be seen
as a major economic partner.

00:23:49.299 --> 00:23:55.340
It already supplies 50 percent of West Germany’s
gas needs; it’s running a trade surplus.

00:23:55.340 --> 00:23:59.149
We should stop thinking of it as just having
20,000 nuclear weapons, but start thinking

00:23:59.149 --> 00:24:01.460
of it as a major trading partner.

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:07.889
Paula Dobriansky: I think that authoritarianism
will reign in Russia in terms of the leadership.

00:24:07.889 --> 00:24:13.260
I think there will still be strong and vibrant
dissident groups trying to change that, but

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:16.059
unfortunately, politically, I see authoritarianism
as reigning.

00:24:16.059 --> 00:24:20.750
Peter Rodman: If Russia gets back on its feet
economically by then, I think it will be more

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:22.440
of a problem than it is otherwise.

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:25.039
Ben Wattenberg: Meaning that it would be a
greater threat.

00:24:25.039 --> 00:24:28.210
Peter Rodman: It’ll be stronger, and I think
we’ll have a bigger problem in foreign policy.

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:30.940
Ben Wattenberg: You’ve managed to come out
pessimistically either way.

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:31.940
Peter Rodman: Yes, yes.

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:32.940
Ben Wattenberg: All right.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:38.460
Thank you, Leon Aron, Peter Rodman, Peter
Rutland, and Paula Dobriansky.

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:41.740
And thank you. We enjoy hearing from our viewers very much.

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:46.100
